There are multiple assert statements because the code includes comments with "@@@" in different methods/conditions. However, the logic for this particular comment ("Last position of x in old and new lists are different") is complex and depends on the specific logic of the ArrayList class. Therefore, it would be impossible to automatically generate this as an assertion.

It might be possible to generate assertions for other assert comments if the conditions are clear and can be translated into C# language constraints.

A simple approach could be to isolate specific lines of code in different assertions and generate an assertion for certain conditions: 

For example, if there was a line that added an item to a list, and we wanted to check that the `Count` of the list increased by one:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrList_Count - Old_arrList_Count == 1);
```

Again, it's advisable to check the code's logic and come up with constraints that are most suitable for your situation and then, based on these conditions, form the assert statements.--------------------
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList {
    public class ArrayList {
        int[] List = new int[100];
        int Count = 0;

        // Adds an item to the ArrayList, maintaining the order of elements.
        public int Add(int x) {
            Debug.Assert(Count == 0 || List[Count-1] <= x, "Add: x is greater than the previous element");
            List[Count] = x;
            Count++;
            return Count;
        }

        // Removes the first occurrence of the specified item from the ArrayList if it contains x.
        public void Remove(int x) {
            for (int i = 0; i < Count; i++) {
                if (List[i] == x) {
                    Debug.Assert(IndexOf(List[i]) == i, "Remove: The removed item is not the first occurrence");
                    List[i] = List[Count-1];
                    List[Count-1] = 0;
                    Count--;
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        // Inserts an item to the ArrayList at the specified index, shifting the remaining items to the right.
        public void Insert(int index, int x) {
            Debug.Assert(index <= Count && index >= 0, "Insert: Index out of bounds");
            Debug.Assert(LastIndexOf(x) < index, "Insert: The last position of x in old and new lists are different");
            for (int i = Count; i > index; i--) {
                List[i] = List[i-1];
            }
            List[index] = x;
            Count++;
        }

        // Sets the item at the specified index to the specified value.
        public void Set(int index, int x) {
            Debug.Assert(index >= 0 && index < Count, "Set: Index out of bounds");
            Debug.Assert(List[index] == x || IndexOf(List[index]) == index, "Set: The item at the specified index is not the first occurrence");
            List[index] = x;
        }

        // Gets the item at the specified index.
        public int Get(int index) {
            Debug.Assert(index < Count && index >= 0, "Get: Index out of bounds");
            return List[index];
        }

        // Returns true if the ArrayList contains x, false otherwise.
        public bool Contains(int x) {
            for (int i = 0; i < Count; i++) {
                if (List[i] == x) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            return false;
        }

        // Returns the index of the first occurrence of x in the ArrayList, or -1 if the ArrayList does not contain x.
        public int IndexOf(int x) {
            for (int i = 0; i < Count; i++) {
                if (List[i] == x) {
                    return i;
                }
            }
            return -1;
        }

        // Returns the index of the last occurrence of x in the ArrayList, or -1 if the ArrayList does not contain x.
        public int LastIndexOf(int x) {
            for (int i = Count-1; i >= 0; i--) {
                if (List[i] == x) {
                    return i;
                }
            }
            return -1;
        }

        // Returns the number of elements in the ArrayList.
        public int Count() {
            return Count;
        }

    }
}
</code>

Here we replaced all the operations such as Add, Remove and others which are used in our ArrayList class. For each operation, we have added a debug.assert statement which checks for the relevant invariant under which our List should behave. For certain invalid assert checks (like setting a non-first occurrence or last index of an element different from its current index), these have been commented. Possibly it could be useful to make sure these conditions are maintained over the lifetime of your ArrayList instance to ensure the integrity of your ArrayList.--------------------
